    <s:String x:Key="ssh_hostkey_first_body" xml:space="preserve">First connection to {0}:{1}.&#10;&#10;Server fingerprint (SHA256):&#10;{2}&#10;&#10;If you know this fingerprint, or you trust the server, continue: it will be remembered for the next connections.</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="ssh_hostkey_continue" xml:space="preserve">Continue</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="ssh_hostkey_trust_continue" xml:space="preserve">Trust and continue</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="ssh_hostkey_other_address_title" xml:space="preserve">Different fingerprint for this address</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="ssh_hostkey_other_address_body" xml:space="preserve">WARNING: the fingerprint of the server {0}:{1} is not the one remembered for this address.&#10;&#10;Remembered:&#10;{2}&#10;&#10;Now:&#10;{3}&#10;&#10;Lemora remembered another fingerprint for this address, but not for this connection: it happens when two servers on different networks (for example of two customers) have the same address. If so, it is normal. Otherwise it may be someone pretending to be the server.</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="ssh_hostkey_changed_title" xml:space="preserve">Server fingerprint changed</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="ssh_hostkey_changed_body" xml:space="preserve">WARNING: the fingerprint of the server {0}:{1} has changed.&#10;&#10;Saved:&#10;{2}&#10;&#10;Now:&#10;{3}&#10;&#10;It may be a reinstalled server or someone pretending to be the server. Continue only if you know why it changed.</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="rdpcert_detail_no_tls_offer" xml:space="preserve">the PC does not offer TLS security (legacy RDP security)</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="rdpcert_detail_refuses_tls" xml:space="preserve">the PC refuses TLS and NLA (legacy RDP security)</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="rdpcert_detail_legacy_chosen" xml:space="preserve">the PC chose the legacy RDP security</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="rdpcert_trust_connect" xml:space="preserve">Trust and connect</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="rdpcert_other_address_title" xml:space="preserve">Different certificate for this address</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="rdpcert_other_address_body" xml:space="preserve">WARNING: the certificate of {0} ({1}:{2}) is not the one remembered for this address.&#10;&#10;Remembered:&#10;{3}&#10;&#10;Now:&#10;{4}&#10;&#10;Lemora remembered another certificate for this address, but not for this connection: it happens when two PCs on different networks (for example of two customers) have the same address. If so, it is normal. Otherwise it may be someone in the middle trying to get the credentials.</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="rdpcert_changed_title" xml:space="preserve">PC certificate changed</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="rdpcert_changed_body" xml:space="preserve">WARNING: the certificate of {0} ({1}:{2}) has changed.&#10;&#10;Remembered:&#10;{3}&#10;&#10;Now:&#10;{4}&#10;&#10;It may be normal (renewed certificate, reinstalled PC) or someone in the middle trying to get the credentials. Continue only if you know why it changed.</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="rdpcert_connect_anyway" xml:space="preserve">Connect anyway</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="rdpcert_notls_pinned_title" xml:space="preserve">The PC no longer uses a protected connection</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="rdpcert_notls_pinned_body" xml:space="preserve">WARNING: {0} ({1}:{2}) had a verified certificate and now answers that it does not use a protected connection.&#10;&#10;It may be someone in the middle trying to get the credentials. Connect only if you know that PC was changed.&#10;&#10;({3})</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="rdpcert_notls_title" xml:space="preserve">Unprotected connection</s:String>
    <s:String x:Key="rdpcert_notls_body" xml:space="preserve">{0} ({1}:{2}) does not use a protected connection (TLS): there is no way to verify you are really talking to that PC, and the password travels less protected.&#10;&#10;({3})&#10;&#10;Connect anyway?</s:String>
